\xi_{j_{1}\cdots j_{q}}\right\vert ^{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{+}%
\end{equation}
and thus defines a posive definite inner product in $\mathcal{A}.$ (One has to
use $^{\dagger}$ and not $^{t}$ as $\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left(  \xi
^{t}\xi\right)  $ is not neccesarily real. Of course one could start with a
real algebra and construct a complex algebra by cannonical complexifications,
then $^{t}$ would be appropiate. $^{\dagger}$ produces a hermitian inner
product, while $^{t}$ a complex symmetric one.)

