Using the $\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}$ functional we can define a complex
Hilbert space $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ with inner product given by
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{HS}%
=\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left\{  A^{\dagger}B\right\}
\end{equation}
and thus norm
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=\left(  \mathit{\operatorname*{tr}%
}\left\{  A^{\dagger}A\right\}  \right)  ^{\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{equation}
(Other positive linear functionals belonging to $\mathcal{A}^{d}$ produce
Hilbert spaces through the GNS construction). The limit of the
$\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}$ functional of a sequence of operators may be
infinite e.g.
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow\infty}\left\{  \mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left\{
A_{j}^{\dagger}A_{j}\right\}  =\infty\right\}  ,
\end{equation}
as not all operators belong to $\mathcal{A}_{HS}.$We can use this Hilbert
space to define an operator norm, (generated by the left regular
representation), $\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}},$ on the $\ast
$-algebra $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ by
\begin{align}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}  &  =\sup\limits_{X\in\mathcal{A}_{HS}%
}\left\{  \frac{\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left\{  \left(  AX\right)
^{\dagger}AX\right\}  }{\mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left\{  X^{\dagger
}X\right\}  }\right\}  =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}%
}=1}\left\{  \operatorname*{tr}\left\{  \left(  AX\right)  ^{\dagger
}AX\right\}  \right\}  =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}%
}=1}\left\{  \operatorname*{tr}\left\{  X^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AX\right\}
\right\}  =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{
\operatorname*{tr}\left\{  A^{\dagger}AXX^{\dagger}\right\}  \right\} \\
&  =\sup\limits_{D\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}}\left\{  \operatorname*{tr}%
\left\{  A^{\dagger}AD\right\}  \right\}  ,
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}$ is the convex set of normal states of
$\mathcal{A}.$

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ wrt the $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert
_{\mathcal{A}}$ norm produces a $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c},$ that has
a normalized trace $\operatorname*{tr}$ (though observe that not all elements
of $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ have finite trace). One should note that the existance of
the normalized trace $\operatorname*{tr}$ is not essential for the
definition/charactrization of $\mathcal{A}^{c},$ it is however essential for
the definition of $\mathcal{A}_{HS}.$ Note that the $c^{\ast}$-algebra of
bounded operators $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right)  $ acting in
$\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ \emph{contains} the space of operators produced by the
action of left multiplication.

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong operator topology induced
by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ and the left regular representation i.e.
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow\infty}\left\{  \mathit{\operatorname*{tr}}\left\{
X^{\dagger}\left(  A-A_{j}\right)  ^{\dagger}\left(  A-A_{j}\right)
X\right\}  \right\}  \forall X=0\equiv s\text{-}\lim\limits_{j}A_{j}=A
\end{equation}
is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra, $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ called the enveloping $w^{\ast}%
$-algebra of the $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c}.$

The norm, $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}},$ can be \emph{extended} to
general linear operators $T\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{A}\right)  $ acting
in $\mathcal{A}$
\begin{equation}
T:\mathcal{A\longrightarrow A}%
\end{equation}
(not just multiplication operators) by%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert
_{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{  \operatorname*{tr}\left\{  T\left(  X\right)
^{\dagger}T\left(  X\right)  \right\}  \right\}  ,
\end{equation}
and even further by
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{A}\right)  }%
=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=1}\left\{
\operatorname*{tr}\left\{  T\left(  X\right)  ^{\dagger}T\left(  X\right)
\right\}  \right\}  .
\end{equation}
This allows one to define the space of bounded operators, $\mathcal{B}\left(
\mathcal{A}\right)  ,$ acting in $\mathcal{A}$ by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{A}\right)  =\left\{  A\left\vert \left\Vert
T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{A}\right)  }<\infty\right.
\right\}  .
\end{equation}


A question one needs to be answered is whether $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ is closed in
the topology induced by the norm $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}$? An
answer can be deduced by the following:

Every Banach space is a closed subspace of its double dual (which by
definition is a closed Banach space) i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{A},
\end{equation}
the Banach space is reflexive if the two spaces are equal and if the space is
not reflexive then $\mathcal{A}$ is a closed subspace of $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}.$ From below
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left(  \mathcal{A}^{c}\right)  ^{\ast\ast}\supseteq
\mathcal{A}^{c}%
\end{equation}
and the Banach space norms in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ are the same.

\subsubsection{Charaterization of $w^{\ast}$-algebras}

A $w^{\ast}$-algebra is characterized by the strong operator closure property
\emph{or equivalently} by

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{the algebraic property}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left(  \mathcal{A}^{w}\right)  ^{\prime\prime}.
\end{equation}
One must define in which set the commutant operation $^{\prime}$ takes place.
It seems that $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right)  $ would be a good candidate.

It is obvious that in general
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}\supseteq\mathcal{A}%
\end{equation}
so the $w^{\ast}$-algebra case is a special case where equality holds,

\item a $c^{\ast}$-algebra that has a predual $\mathcal{A}_{\ast},$which is a
Banach space.
\end{enumerate}

A link between $w^{\ast}$ and $c^{\ast}$ algebras is that if $\mathcal{A}$ is
a non-degenerate $c^{\ast}$-algebra then its double dual $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}$ is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra. In general
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}


Thus
\begin{equation}
\left(  \mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\right)  ^{\prime\prime}=\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}%
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{A}$ is a $c^{\ast}$-algebra, showing that $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}$ is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra.

$\lceil$ It is always true that
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime\prime}  &  =\mathcal{A}^{\prime}\\
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}  &  =\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}%
\end{align}
so $\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime},$ the double commutant of $\mathcal{A},$ is
always a $w^{\ast}$-algebra. However in general
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast\ast}\neq\mathcal{A}^{\ast}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

The \emph{Sherman--Takeda theorem} states that if $\mathcal{A}$ is a $c^{\ast
}$-algebra then its double dual is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra, and any homomorphism
of $\mathcal{A}$ to a $w^{\ast}$-algebra factors through its double dual. The
theorem was announced by \emph{Sherman} (1950) and proved by \emph{Takeda}
(1954). The double dual of $\mathcal{A}$ is called the universal enveloping
$w^{\ast}$-algebra of $\mathcal{A}$. By the Sherman--Takeda theorem, the
double dual of a $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$, $\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}$,
can be identified with $\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}$ as Banach spaces i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}\overset{Banach}{=}\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$As an example
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  ^{\prime}  &  =\left\{  I\right\} \\
\left\{  I\right\}  ^{\prime}  &  =\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)
^{\prime\prime}=\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  ,
\end{align}
where the cummutators are taken in $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)
.$Thus%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)   &  =\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}%
\right)  ^{\ast\ast}\text{ (thefore reflexive)}\\
\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  ^{\ast}  &  =\mathcal{B}_{1}\left(
\mathcal{H}\right)  ^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{B}_{1}\left(  \mathcal{H}%
\right)  .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

The double commutant $\left(  \mathcal{A}^{c}\right)  ^{\prime\prime}$ is a
$w^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ which in general does not have a
normalized trace and is the closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong,
weak, ultra strong and ultraweak topologies in $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ induced
by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$. It is also closed in the norm topology that is the same
in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}.$

