\paragraph{\emph{Partial }Isometries in c$^{\ast}$and w$^{\ast}$-Algebras}

If $QQ^{\dagger}$ is an orthogonal projector (self adjoint idempotent) then
both $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger}$ are a partial isometries and $Q^{\dagger}Q$ is also
an orthogonal projector, moreover this is equivalent to
\begin{equation}
Q^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}=Q^{\dagger}\Leftrightarrow QQ^{\dagger}Q=Q,
\end{equation}
which can be thought of as a projections from the left or eigenvalue
equations
\begin{align}
\left(  QQ^{\dagger}\right)  Q  & =Q\\
\left(  Q^{\dagger}Q\right)  Q^{\dagger}  & =Q^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
These operations could also be written as acting from the right i.e.
\begin{align}
Q\left(  Q^{\dagger}Q\right)    & =Q\\
Q^{\dagger}\left(  QQ^{\dagger}\right)    & =Q^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
Observe that $\left\{  \left.  \eta Q,\eta Q^{\dagger}\right\vert \eta
\in\mathbb{C}\right\}  $ are also eigenvectors with the same eigenvalues.
These equations can be generalized to
\begin{align}
\left(  QQ^{\dagger}\right)  QA  & =QA\\
\left(  Q^{\dagger}Q\right)  Q^{\dagger}A  & =Q^{\dagger}A
\end{align}
for any $A\in\mathcal{F}.$

The projectors $QQ^{\dagger}$ and $Q^{\dagger}Q$ are said to be equivalent and
this defines an equivalence relationship on the set of orthogonal projectors
(self adjoint idempotents) contained in $\mathcal{F}$. 

